On the tenth day of my internship, I started learning 
about Linux file permissions. I read the related sections 
from the Linux book and learned about the three basic 
permission types: read, write, and execute. I also learned 
that Linux permissions are defined separately for the file 
owner, the group, and other users.

I studied how to examine file permissions using the ls -l 
command and learned how to interpret permission expressions 
such as rwxr-xr--. I tried to understand what each character 
represents and how permissions affect access to files 
and directories.

I also studied the basic usage of the chmod command. 
The topic helped me understand an important part of
Linux system security and showed me why controlling 
access to files is necessary in a multi-user operating system.
